Summarize the GM narration into a compact log line, decide whether the chronicle should close, and decide whether the active scene resolved.

Write the summary as a single sentence of at most 180 characters capturing who acted, what changed, and where it happened, in plain declarative prose free of markdown and quotes. When a skill check resolved, close the sentence with a short parenthetical outcome such as (advance) or (collapse).

The BEATS section lists the chronicle's open goals, and those goals are the primary stakes. `shouldCloseChronicle` is true when the narration clearly resolves them or a wrap request reaches its final turn, and false in every other case. A satisfied intent — a plan made, a question answered, a route charted, a single scene finished — leaves the chronicle open, as does any narration that asks the player a question or offers a next choice. While open beats remain the chronicle stays open; once every beat has reached an end and the narration reads as an ending, closing becomes appropriate. When the call is close, answer false: chronicles end at wrap requests far more often than on their own.

The SCENE section carries the active scene with its clock. `sceneOutcome` stays `continue` while the scene's own completion rules remain unsatisfied. Answer `complete` when the typed scene reaches its end even though the chronicle continues, and treat a full clock (`clockFull` true) as the scene having spent its runway: resolve it now, in whichever direction the narration supports. Answer `abandoned` when the scene's premise has dissolved — the story left the place or dropped the pursuit the scene was about, which a gap between `startedAtLocation` and `currentLocation` often shows. Give one short sentence in `sceneOutcomeReason` whenever the outcome is `complete` or `abandoned`, and null otherwise. With no SCENE section, answer `continue` with a null reason.
